# Round 3: escalation results (negative-ish). Final recommendation.

Bugs GPT flagged in escalate.py were fixed (mask shapes; unsuccessful fast runs now always escalate; edge cases).
Data: p=0.004, 3000 pairs, split 50/25/25 train/val/test by pair; thresholds picked on validation as the fastest
policy with val pair failures <= Relay-BP's. TEST (750 pairs):
```
BP-OSD (kit)                   fails 46/750  LER/cycle 5.26e-03  mean 381 ms  p50 425  p99 1321
Relay-BP (kit)                 fails 12/750  LER/cycle 1.34e-03  mean 361 ms  p50 112  p99 5630
Relay-fast alone               fails 25/750  LER/cycle 2.82e-03  mean  71 ms  p50  16  p99 1035
rule: escalate if no solution  fails 16/750                      mean 168 ms           p99 6380
ML gate (LR on rf_* diag.)     fails 17/750                      mean 265 ms           p99 6397  (4.7% escalated)
rule: iters<=K & cost<=W       fails 13/750                      mean 312 ms           p99 6573
oracle gate                    fails  8/750                      mean 128 ms           p99 2114
```
Reading (Claude): the single-run diagnostics of relay_fast cannot tell when its (valid) solution is in the wrong
logical class; validation has ~12 failures so threshold selection is noisy. Oracle headroom is ~2.8x.

Now running (results soon): **agreement gate** — two relay_fast runs with seeds 0 and 1; if both succeed and predict the
same logical observables, accept; else run full Relay-BP (or Relay-BP stop_nconv=2). No learned parameters.

Questions (under ~350 words):
1. Is the agreement gate the right next idea? Pitfalls (e.g. both legs share the same deterministic first leg, so
   easy shots always "agree" — is that a problem)?
2. Where should ML go in the final pipeline so it is *genuinely* useful rather than decorative? One concrete proposal.
3. One-paragraph final recommendation for the team for the remaining hackathon time.

codex
1. **Yes—agreement is the right next experiment**, because it adds information that single-run confidence diagnostics lack. But agreement is evidence, not a correctness certificate: two runs can return the same wrong logical class. If both exit through the same deterministic first leg, changing seeds provides no additional evidence on those shots. That is harmless when the shared answer is correct, but leaves common-mode failures undetected. Record failures separately for shared-first-leg agreement, agreement after randomized search, and disagreement. Verify that seeds actually change the randomized search, and compare the entire predicted logical-observable vector. Measure end-to-end latency, including both fast runs and fallback; shared computation may help. Treat `stop_nconv=2` as a separate decoder requiring its own accuracy measurement.

2. **Put ML in compute allocation, with a concrete target: choosing the second-run configuration.** Using the syndrome and first-run diagnostics, select among a small, fixed menu of genuinely different perturbation schedules or budgets. Train on paired outcomes to favor configurations that expose first-run logical errors through disagreement, penalizing added latency. This directly targets the missing diversity rather than asking a classifier to infer logical correctness from weak confidence signals. Keep the agreement/fallback rule fixed, and benchmark against the best fixed second-run configuration. This is useful only if it improves held-out accuracy versus latency; configuration-specific outcomes would require additional training data.

3. **For the remaining hackathon time, prioritize a clean agreement experiment and a credible conclusion.** Keep full Relay-BP as the reference; report paired failure transitions, escalation rate, mean latency, and tail latency for agreement with each fallback. The current 13-versus-12 result does not establish equivalent reliability, and validation selection with so few failures is unstable. Avoid further tuning on the existing test set; use fresh evaluation if feasible. If agreement delivers a convincing tradeoff, make it the main result. Otherwise, present the negative gating result and oracle headroom honestly, with learned diversity selection as future work. Do not force ML into the submission without a measured contribution.
